  6	\section{Non-projective Output}
  7	\label{sec:self-improvement}
  8	
  9	A large part of this chapter is devoted to proving
 10	Lemma~\ref{lem:self-improvement-helper}, which is a slightly weaker form of the
 11	projective self-improvement theorem proved in Section~\ref{sec:self-improvement-projective}.
 12	The key difference is that the output family~$H$ is only required to be a
 13	submeasurement, rather than a projective submeasurement. Once this non-projective
 14	statement is available, Theorem~\ref{thm:orthonormalization} upgrades~$H$ to a
 15	projective family without losing control of the four quantitative conclusions.
 16	
 17	Compared with the projective theorem, the helper lemma records strong
 18	self-consistency using the explicit inequality from
 19	Definition~\ref{def:strong-self-consistency}, keeps the boundedness conclusion in
 20	terms of an auxiliary operator~$Z$, and produces a much smaller error parameter.
 21	
 22	\begin{lemma}[Self-improvement with non-projective output]\label{lem:self-improvement-helper}
 23	  \lean{MIPStarRE.LDT.SelfImprovement.selfImprovementHelper}
 24	  \lean{MIPStarRE.LDT.SelfImprovement.self_improvement_helper_with_slackness_of_sdp_statement_with_slackness}
 25	  \leanok
 26	  \uses{def:polymeasurements, def:simeq, def:strong-self-consistency}
 27	  Let $G \in \polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ be a measurement with the following property:
 28	  \begin{enumerate}
 29	    \item (Consistency with~$A$): \label{item:self-improvement-G-consistency} On average over $u \sim \F_q^{m}$,
 30	    \[
 90	    = \E_{u} \sum_h H_h^u
 91	    \le I,
 92	  \]
 93	  so $H$ is also a submeasurement and therefore lies in~$\polysub{m}{q}{d}$.
 94	
